   "id": "1-foundational-axioms-dtft-convergence-barrier-the-complex-plane",
   "title": "1. Foundational Axioms: DTFT Convergence Barrier & The Complex z-Plane",
   "blocks": [
    {
     "t": "img",
     "src": "/notes/ssf/figures_ch6/fig_01_dtft_to_z_transform_mapping_engine.jpg",
     "alt": "Figure 6.1: DTFT to Z-Transform Mapping Engine"
    },
    {
     "t": "h3",
     "text": "1.1 The DTFT Convergence Barrier"
    },
    {
     "t": "p",
     "text": "The Discrete-Time Fourier Transform (DTFT) of a sequence $x[n]$ is defined by:"
    },
    {
     "t": "math",
     "tex": "X(e^{j\\Omega}) = \\sum_{n=-\\infty}^{\\infty} x[n] e^{-j\\Omega n}"
    },
    {
     "t": "p",
     "text": "A necessary and sufficient condition for the uniform convergence of $X(e^{j\\Omega})$ is the **absolute summability** of $x[n]$:"
    },
    {
     "t": "math",
     "tex": "\\sum_{n=-\\infty}^{\\infty} \\vert x[n]\\vert < \\infty"
    },
    {
     "t": "p",
     "text": "*Failure Modes of DTFT:*"
    },
    {
     "t": "ul",
     "items": [
      "Exponentially growing sequences: $a^n u[n]$ with $\\vert a\\vert \\ge 1$ (e.g., $2^n u[n]$).",
      "Linearly growing sequences: Ramp $n u[n]$, quadratic $n^2 u[n]$.",
      "Unstable discrete-time LTI system impulse responses."
     ]
    },
    {
     "t": "h3",
     "text": "1.2 The Convergence Factor Damping ($r^{-n}$)"
    },
    {
     "t": "p",
     "text": "To generalize the DTFT to growing and non-summable sequences, we multiply $x[n]$ by a real decaying exponential damping factor $r^{-n}$ (where $r = \\vert z\\vert > 0$):"
    },
    {
     "t": "math",
     "tex": "x_{\\text{damped}}[n] = x[n] \\cdot r^{-n}"
    },
    {
     "t": "p",
     "text": "Taking the DTFT of $x_{\\text{damped}}[n]$:"
    },
    {
     "t": "math",
     "tex": "X_{\\text{damped}}(e^{j\\Omega}) = \\sum_{n=-\\infty}^{\\infty} \\left( x[n] r^{-n} \\right) e^{-j\\Omega n} = \\sum_{n=-\\infty}^{\\infty} x[n] \\left( r e^{j\\Omega} \\right)^{-n}"
    },
    {
     "t": "p",
     "text": "Defining the complex frequency variable $z$:"
    },
    {
     "t": "math",
     "tex": "z \\triangleq r e^{j\\Omega} = \\vert z\\vert e^{j \\angle z}"
    },
    {
     "t": "h3",
     "text": "1.3 Bilateral (Two-Sided) Z-Transform Definitions"
    },
    {
     "t": "ul",
     "items": [
      "**Analysis Equation (Forward Z-Transform):**"
     ]
    },
    {
     "t": "math",
     "tex": "X(z) \\triangleq \\mathcal{Z}\\{x[n]\\} = \\sum_{n=-\\infty}^{\\infty} x[n] z^{-n}"
    },
    {
     "t": "ul",
     "items": [
      "**Synthesis Equation (Inverse Z-Transform):**"
     ]
    },
    {
     "t": "math",
     "tex": "x[n] = \\mathcal{Z}^{-1}\\{X(z)\\} = \\frac{1}{2\\pi j} \\oint_{C} X(z) z^{n-1} \\, dz"
    },
    {
     "t": "p",
     "text": "where $C$ is a counterclockwise closed contour residing entirely within the Region of Convergence (ROC) and encircling the origin $z = 0$."
    },
    {
     "t": "h3",
     "text": "1.4 Connection Between Z-Transform and DTFT"
    },
    {
     "t": "p",
     "text": "Evaluating the Z-transform on the **Unit Circle** ($\\vert z\\vert = 1 \\implies r = 1 \\implies z = e^{j\\Omega}$):"
    },
    {
     "t": "math",
     "tex": "X(z)\\Big\\vert_{z = e^{j\\Omega}} = \\sum_{n=-\\infty}^{\\infty} x[n] e^{-j\\Omega n} = X(e^{j\\Omega}) = \\text{DTFT}\\{x[n]\\}"
    },
    {
     "t": "p",
     "text": "**Fundamental Theorem:** The DTFT of $x[n]$ exists if and only if the Region of Convergence (ROC) of $X(z)$ strictly contains the Unit Circle $\\vert z\\vert = 1$."
    }
   ]
